WEBVTT

00:00:01.800 --> 00:00:06.877
The lab's loop avoids writing 2 times x times y, and saves multiplications.

00:00:07.230 --> 00:00:11.817
Does it do fewer arithmetic operations per repetition than the textbook version?

00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:13.099
Yes or no.

00:00:13.450 --> 00:00:14.581
Pause and write it.

00:00:22.730 --> 00:00:28.481
The textbook squares a pair (a, b) into (a times a minus b times b, 2 times a times

00:00:28.481 --> 00:00:31.391
b), then adds the middleman pair (cx, cy).

00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:34.471
The lab code never writes 2 times a times b.

00:00:34.830 --> 00:00:38.926
Instead it keeps an extra number called w and subtracts things from it.

00:00:39.270 --> 00:00:43.622
I read it and objected: the lab says "square", and then the code subtracts.

00:00:43.970 --> 00:00:47.213
The objection was right; the code does not match the words.

00:00:47.570 --> 00:00:49.490
Where did 2 times a times b go?

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:55.512
The tools needed are multiplying out one square by hand, and something I already

00:00:55.512 --> 00:00:57.072
knew: i times i is −1.

00:00:57.420 --> 00:01:01.879
Then comes the question a reviewer should ask about any clever rewrite: what did it

00:01:01.879 --> 00:01:03.329
save, and what did it cost?

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:07.264
Is counting multiplications even the right way to answer that?

00:01:14.410 --> 00:01:20.696
Lines C, D and F of one repetition write x2, y2 and w; lines A and B of the next

00:01:20.696 --> 00:01:22.346
repetition read them.

00:01:22.700 --> 00:01:25.943
Call the x and y of the earlier repetition a and b.

00:01:26.290 --> 00:01:36.423
Then x2 = a·a, y2 = b·b, z = a + b, and w = (a + b) × (a + b).

00:01:36.770 --> 00:01:37.538
Pause.

00:01:37.890 --> 00:01:41.367
Multiply it out term by term, then put it into line B.

00:01:47.720 --> 00:01:54.739
(a + b)(a + b) = a·a + 2·a·b + b·b.

00:01:55.090 --> 00:02:00.935
In line B the a·a and b·b cancel, leaving 2·a·b + cy.

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:04.800
Line A is a·a − b·b + cx.

00:02:05.150 --> 00:02:07.902
The textbook formula is all there, rearranged.

00:02:08.250 --> 00:02:16.237
The i in "i times i is −1" is not the loop counter i, so I write it as capital I: I

00:02:16.237 --> 00:02:17.103
× I = −1.

00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:20.873
Write the pair (a, b) as a single number, a + b·I.

00:02:21.220 --> 00:02:21.988
Pause.

00:02:22.340 --> 00:02:30.212
Multiply (a + b·I) by (a + b·I) and replace I × I by −1.

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:41.893
The result is a·a − b·b + 2·a·b·I.

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:47.872
Line A is the part without I, plus cx; line B is the part with I, plus cy.

00:02:48.230 --> 00:02:54.289
A repetition is therefore: square a + b·I, then add cx + cy·I.

00:02:54.640 --> 00:02:55.408
Pause.

00:03:01.750 --> 00:03:19.406
Repetition 1 gives x = −0.75, y = −1.25, x2 = 0.5625, y2 = 1.5625, z = −2.0, w =

00:03:19.406 --> 00:03:20.289
4.0.

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:29.152
In repetition 2, line B and the textbook both give 0.625, and x = −1.75.

00:03:29.500 --> 00:03:33.767
In repetition 3 both give −3.4375.

00:03:34.120 --> 00:03:46.045
Before repetition 4 the check is 3.693604 + 11.816406 = 15.51, so iters = 3.

00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:48.747
Here is the loop written straight from the textbook.

00:03:49.090 --> 00:03:52.141
The check multiplies x by x and y by y.

00:03:52.490 --> 00:03:57.541
The new first number goes into a temporary, xt, because the next line still needs

00:03:57.541 --> 00:03:58.165
the old x.

00:03:58.520 --> 00:04:02.829
The new second number uses the old x and y, and then x is replaced.

00:04:04.980 --> 00:04:10.267
Count each multiply and each add or subtract in one repetition, including the check,

00:04:10.267 --> 00:04:15.428
and the longest chain from the values at the top of a repetition to the values the

00:04:15.428 --> 00:04:16.372
next one needs.

00:04:16.720 --> 00:04:22.941
On my GTX 1650 each of these operations delivers its value 4 cycles after it begins;

00:04:22.941 --> 00:04:28.496
that is the published microbenchmark figure, and my own measurement agrees.

00:04:28.850 --> 00:04:29.618
Pause.

00:04:29.960 --> 00:04:30.984
Fill the table.

00:04:37.340 --> 00:04:44.332
The straight version does 6 multiplies and 4 adds, 10 in total, with a longest chain

00:04:44.332 --> 00:04:48.327
of 3: x, then x·x, then minus y·y, then plus cx.

00:04:48.670 --> 00:04:56.357
The lab version does 3 multiplies and 7 adds or subtracts, also 10, with a longest

00:04:56.357 --> 00:05:01.513
chain of 5: w, minus x2, minus y2, plus cy, x + y, z·z.

00:05:01.870 --> 00:05:05.390
Along the chain that is 12 cycles of waiting against 20.

00:05:05.740 --> 00:05:06.508
Pause.

00:05:06.850 --> 00:05:10.711
Write a sentence on when the lab's rewrite is a win and when it is not.

00:05:17.070 --> 00:05:22.232
The rewrite moves work from multiplications to additions without lowering the total,

00:05:22.232 --> 00:05:26.350
so it wins only where a multiplication costs more than an addition.

00:05:26.700 --> 00:05:32.033
Where every operation costs the same 4 cycles, it lengthens the chain from 3 to 5.

00:05:32.380 --> 00:05:37.155
Which version is faster on this GPU is a measurement question, not a counting

00:05:37.155 --> 00:05:37.713
question.

00:05:38.060 --> 00:05:42.958
The tempting answer, "fewer multiplications means faster", fails because the total

00:05:42.958 --> 00:05:43.735
stayed at 10.

00:05:45.890 --> 00:05:50.007
Code that has been tuned often stops looking like the formula it computes.

00:05:50.350 --> 00:05:55.172
The tuning was done for a reason, on some machine, at some time, and the reason may

00:05:55.172 --> 00:05:56.857
not hold on the next machine.

00:05:57.210 --> 00:06:01.982
Reading such code means recovering the formula first, then asking which cost the

00:06:01.982 --> 00:06:03.653
rewrite was trading against.

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:08.757
For a manager: "we optimized it" is a claim about one cost on one machine.

00:06:09.110 --> 00:06:11.137
Ask which cost, and on which machine.

00:06:13.290 --> 00:06:14.463
The answer is no.

00:06:14.810 --> 00:06:20.834
Both versions do 10 operations per repetition: the textbook does 6 multiplications

00:06:20.834 --> 00:06:23.258
and 4 additions, the lab 3 and 7.

00:06:23.610 --> 00:06:24.655
Mark your tally.

00:06:26.800 --> 00:06:31.626
How would you decide, by measurement and not by counting, which of the two loop

00:06:31.626 --> 00:06:33.520
versions is faster on this GPU?

00:06:33.870 --> 00:06:34.638
Pause.

00:06:40.990 --> 00:06:43.891
Write both as GPU kernels of the same shape.

00:06:44.240 --> 00:06:49.036
Time each at two loop lengths, with clock reads tied to the values, and subtract

00:06:49.036 --> 00:06:50.235
within each version.

00:06:50.590 --> 00:06:55.630
Then read both machine listings to see what the compiler fused into multiply-adds,

00:06:55.630 --> 00:06:57.843
because that changes the real chain.

